WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.040
He-Man has become a pop culture icon, with his&nbsp;
magic sword defending his home planet of Eternia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.080
But, did you know he came from Star Wars&nbsp;
toys, or that he once became Shazam?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.920
Here's the untold truth about He-Man&nbsp;
and the Masters of the Universe.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.000
The 1980s saw a major explosion in action figures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.400
This was largely due to the "Star Wars"&nbsp;
film franchise and the popular figurines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.440
of characters like Luke Skywalker and Darth&nbsp;
Vader made by Kenner Toys. Surprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.040
however, Kenner wasn't the first toy company that&nbsp;
received an offer to produce "Star Wars" toys.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:41.200
In 1976, Ray Wagner, the CEO of Mattel&nbsp;
Incorporated, declined George Lucas' asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:48.880
price of $750,000 to produce a toy line of "Star&nbsp;
Wars" action figures. Wagner undoubtedly ended up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.680
regretting the decision when the toys became one&nbsp;
of the most lucrative parts of the film franchise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.440
Mattel then quickly launched several medieval and&nbsp;
science fiction-themed toy lines of their own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.440
They hoped to create the next&nbsp;
hit action figure in the bargain.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.480
According to the book "Mastering the Universe:&nbsp;
He-Man and the Rise and Fall of a Billion-Dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.080
Idea," Roger Sweet, a designer at Mattel,&nbsp;
decided on the generic name "He-Man" to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.800
a toy line around. Although none of the aspects&nbsp;
of He-Man's mythology had been developed yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.760
the simplicity of the name was key. This&nbsp;
gave the company's team of designers a mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.160
blank canvas that they could use to create&nbsp;
virtually any story world they imagined.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.160
Today, it's impossible to separate the name&nbsp;
"He-Man" from the image of a massively muscled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.400
blonde barbarian. But when Mattel&nbsp;
began developing the He-Man concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.320
the toy line could have gone in&nbsp;
any number of different directions.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.600
At one point, Mattel executives were given a&nbsp;
choice between three prototype models of He-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.280
a barbarian, a soldier,&nbsp;
and a science-fiction hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.600
Weirdly, the sci-fi version of He-Man&nbsp;
wore a helmet that looked very much like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.480
the Mandalorian helmet worn by Boba Fett in&nbsp;
"The Empire Strikes Back." But the helmet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.280
reappeared in a recent version of Vykron in&nbsp;
the Masters of the Universe Classics line.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.440
Ultimately, the barbarian version of He-Man proved&nbsp;
the most appealing to the executives. The "He-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.880
and the Masters of the Universe" backstory then&nbsp;
went on to become a sword-and-sorcery epic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.360
However, it's strange to think that, at one&nbsp;
point, He-Man could have been Mattel's version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.000
of a Mandalorian. Lucasfilm likely wouldn't&nbsp;
care for the design though, so it's lucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.880
that He-Man decided to bare his distinctive&nbsp;
pageboy haircut and tanned face to the world.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.040
Seeing how "He-Man and the Masters&nbsp;
of the Universe" developed from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.520
toy line into an animated series is interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.400
because it reveals how He-Man's story&nbsp;
world has been regularly reimagined.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.480
Mattel included a mini-comic with its&nbsp;
action figures that helped flesh out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.080
characters' backstories. Originally, He-Man was&nbsp;
a barbarian from an Eternian tribe that existed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:57.840
in a post-apocalyptic world. Armed with advanced&nbsp;
weaponry, He-Man defended Castle Grayskull from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.360
his enemy Skeletor. Interestingly, He-Man and&nbsp;
Skeletor both possessed half of the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:08.480
Power Sword, which reflected the way kids could&nbsp;
combine the action figure swords into one.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.840
By 1983, Mattel had partnered with Filmation&nbsp;
to develop a He-Man cartoon that provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.320
He-Man's most popular origin. Now&nbsp;
he was Prince Adam of Eternia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.920
who had been endowed with the power to transform&nbsp;
into He-Man using the Power Sword. This power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:28.720
was given to him by The Sorceress of Castle&nbsp;
Grayskull. Adam could also turn his cowardly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.880
pet Cringer into the fearsome Battle-Cat, and&nbsp;
fought with his allies against Skeletor's forces.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:39.440
Other cartoons continued tweaking He-Man's&nbsp;
origin. In "The New Adventures of He-Man,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.560
He-Man was summoned to the&nbsp;
future to defend mankind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.240
reviving his science fiction roots. In the 2002&nbsp;
"He-Man and the Masters of the Universe" reboot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.760
Adam was a spoiled teen who reluctantly became&nbsp;
He-Man to rescue his kingdom from Skeletor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:59.040
While most animated series keep the most popular&nbsp;
beats, there's always room for some revision.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.000
He-Man may be Eternia's greatest champion,&nbsp;
but he has roots in another world:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.520
Earth. According to the 1980s cartoon series,&nbsp;
Prince Adam's mother Queen Marlena was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:13.760
astronaut from Earth who crash-landed&nbsp;
on Eternia and ended up marrying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.200
planet's King Randor. One episode, "The Rainbow&nbsp;
Warrior," referenced Marlena's piloting skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.560
but she was mostly relegated&nbsp;
to being a background player.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.560
In 1985, the made-for-TV animated film "He-Man&nbsp;
&amp; She-Ra: A Christmas Special" was released.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.440
In the film, Grayskull's jester Orko accidentally&nbsp;
travels to Earth during Christmas time.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.640
"Something tells me I'm in big trouble."

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.440
The Eternian magician brings back a couple&nbsp;
of Earth children, causing Queen Marlena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:46.240
to reminisce about Christmas. Prince Adam&nbsp;
actually got to play Santa Claus at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:49.840
showing how Marlena had been&nbsp;
influencing Eternia's culture.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.760
Most interestingly, however, it turns&nbsp;
out the Earth that Marlena is from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:59.360
also exists in the DC Universe. This&nbsp;
can be seen in "DC Comics Presents #47"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:05.040
where He-Man acknowledges Marlena told him stories&nbsp;
about Superman, the protector of her home-world.

00:05:05.697 --> 00:05:10.960
Both He-Man and his alter ego Prince Adam were voiced&nbsp;
by John Erwin in the 1980s cartoon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.320
Erwin gave He-Man a majestic, authoritative voice&nbsp;
while giving Adam a more laid-back attitude.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.160
"We have no time to lose."

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.480
"By the power of Grayskull!"

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.960
This helped differentiate the two&nbsp;
personas, even though the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.480
original character designs made the two of them&nbsp;
look identical save for He-Man's darker tan.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.760
Beyond He-Man, Erwin has an impressive list of&nbsp;
live-action and voice acting credits to his name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.040
including roles in "Rawhide," "Back to the Future&nbsp;
II," and "Babe." However, his most famous role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:47.680
was "Morris the Cat," the feline spokesperson&nbsp;
and face of 9-Lives cat food during the 1970s.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.080
Yep, the voice of He-Man got famous&nbsp;
delivering snarky lines as a fat orange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.840
cat who rivaled Garfield in&nbsp;
deadpan humor with lines like,

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.520
"Who says I need improving?"

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.640
The He-Man cartoon probably missed out on a&nbsp;
golden opportunity to also cast Erwin as He-Man's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.440
feline steed Battle-Cat. But he was voiced&nbsp;
by celebrated voice actor Alan Oppenheimer.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:13.280
He-Man may look like a warrior straight out of the&nbsp;
sword-and-sorcery epics of Conan the Barbarian.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:19.280
But the 1980s cartoon went out of its way to show&nbsp;
him dealing with problems non-violently. While he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.080
was capable of lifting mountains and pushing a&nbsp;
moon out of orbit, He-Man rarely used his great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.360
strength to hurt people. Plus, he usually limited&nbsp;
his punching targets to robots or buildings.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.560
Many episodes showed him using diplomacy to&nbsp;
convince potential enemies to change their ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.160
He even got Daimar the Demon&nbsp;
King to reconsider invading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.800
Eternia in the episode "Daimar the Demon."

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.120
A lot of this is likely due to the producers&nbsp;
wanting parents to believe the cartoon would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.080
teach their children good values, so they could&nbsp;
keep watching the show and buying the toys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.080
Much like "G.I. Joe," the episodes&nbsp;
even regularly ended with a moral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:57.920
Prince Adam, Teela, or the other&nbsp;
characters instructed viewers on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:01.840
the importance of telling the truth or&nbsp;
working problems out by asking for help.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.960
Superheroes typically wear a symbol or emblem&nbsp;
that represents their power — from Superman's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:13.120
"S"-shaped family crest to Batman's bat totem. In&nbsp;
He-Man's case, the character designers initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:18.160
placed an Iron Cross in the center of his&nbsp;
chest harness to enhance his distinctive look.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:23.920
The problem was, the Iron Cross was appropriated&nbsp;
by the Nazi party who placed a swastika symbol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:29.520
in its center. Notably, the swastika itself is an&nbsp;
ancient symbol that had been used as a symbol of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.280
well-being by many societies before Adolf Hitler&nbsp;
appropriated it for the Nazi flag. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.240
any positive connotations of these symbols&nbsp;
have been overshadowed by their negative use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.880
And, they continue to be employed as a&nbsp;
hate symbol by extremist groups today.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.560
Naturally, He-Man didn't wear&nbsp;
a swastika on his Iron Cross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.120
and being a hero of the planet Eternia, the&nbsp;
symbol likely meant something different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:58.000
But it was eventually realized that his chest&nbsp;
emblem could get people thinking the wrong thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.280
Because of this, more modern depictions of He-Man&nbsp;
have modified the Iron Cross into an "H" symbol.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:08.720
Mattel came out with a lot of creative action&nbsp;
figures when it developed the original He-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.760
toy line. But, the weirdest characters were&nbsp;
members of Skeletor's entourage. Many of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:19.360
particularly Evil-Lyn and Beast Man, went on&nbsp;
to become major figures in the animated series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:25.040
One action figure was so ridiculous the story&nbsp;
editors vowed to never use him in the cartoon.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:30.480
That would be "Stinkor," a humanoid skunk whose&nbsp;
"power" was that he smelled really bad and gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:36.400
off toxic odors. Mattel manufactured the toy&nbsp;
to intentionally smell bad by treating it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:42.720
musky-smelling patchouli oil. And then it earned&nbsp;
a reputation for stinking up toy boxes nationwide.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.200
Stinkor's action figure also came with a&nbsp;
mini-comic, "The Stench of Evil," where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:52.560
attacked He-Man with his horrible body odor, but&nbsp;
when the character was presented to the Filmation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.960
staff, they reportedly burst out laughing&nbsp;
and decided to keep him out of their scripts.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:01.680
Stinkor finally made it to television&nbsp;
in the 2002 "He-Man" cartoon reboot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:07.040
where it was revealed that he was once a thief&nbsp;
who became mutated by a chemical accident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:11.680
Notably, Stinkor isn't nose blind to&nbsp;
his stink and must wear an oxygen mask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.560
making his ability one of the&nbsp;
worst superpowers to have.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.840
While He-Man was marketed toward boys, girls&nbsp;
liked the "Masters of the Universe" too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:24.880
Mattel decided to capitalize on this&nbsp;
by creating He-Man's twin sister Adora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:30.640
a.k.a "She-Ra." She was given her own toy line&nbsp;
and an animated series called "She-Ra: Princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:36.240
of Power." She-Ra defended the world of Etheria&nbsp;
from the Evil Horde after being given a "Sword of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.440
Protection" that imbued her with superhuman&nbsp;
strength, speed, durability, and agility.&nbsp;

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.800
Although She-Ra was established&nbsp;
as He-Man's physical equal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.360
she may have been his superior in at least&nbsp;
one respect — her "Sword of Protection"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:54.960
could transform into anything she required.&nbsp;
Various episodes of her cartoon showed She-Ra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.400
turning the sword into a rope, a shield, a&nbsp;
boomerang, a magnet, and even an ice maker.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:04.080
Frankly, by the time She-Ra started&nbsp;
turning her sword into a parachute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.520
things got a little ridiculous.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.800
While fans may now look back on the original&nbsp;
"He-Man" animated series with fondness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:14.080
many admit the cartoon's plots&nbsp;
could get a little silly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.920
Filmation managed to get some serious&nbsp;
talent working on these episodes, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:23.120
and many of their staff members went on to&nbsp;
have major careers in DC and Marvel Comics.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.520
Bruce Timm and Paul Dini, the minds&nbsp;
behind the DC Animated Universe that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:31.440
included the award-winning "Batman:&nbsp;
The Animated Series," "Batman Beyond,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:35.600
and "Justice League Unlimited," got&nbsp;
their start on the He-Man cartoon.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.800
Timm worked as a background artist and layout&nbsp;
designer who also wrote He-Man mini comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.360
Dini was a script writer for the cartoon and wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:47.920
episodes such as "Quest for He-Man,"&nbsp;
where He-Man was rendered amnesic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.880
banished to a contaminated planet to&nbsp;
teach kids about the dangers of pollution.

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:56.880
Likewise, J. Michael Straczynski, who&nbsp;
had runs on "The Amazing Spider-Man"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.600
and "Fantastic Four" for Marvel Comics,&nbsp;
served as the story editor for both "He-Man"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.240
and "She-Ra." It might not be as slick&nbsp;
as today's animated series, but for many,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.360
"He-Man and the Masters of the Universe" helped&nbsp;
pave the modern age of superhero animated shows.

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:15.440
He-Man may be "the most powerful man&nbsp;
in the universe," but how does he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:19.120
stack up against the other "most&nbsp;
powerful hero in the universe,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:24.080
Superman? As it turns out, the comics have&nbsp;
addressed this question multiple times.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:30.640
"DC Comics Presents" #47 saw Superman transported&nbsp;
to Eternia by a mystic cloud where he met up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:35.680
He-Man. Although the two started out as allies,&nbsp;
Skeletor was able to take magical control of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:41.120
Superman and pit him against He-Man. While&nbsp;
it looked like Superman had the upper hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:45.120
he was able to shake off Skeletor's&nbsp;
control and break off his attack.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:49.920
However, Superman and He-Man had a much&nbsp;
more brutal fight in the "Injustice vs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:55.680
Masters of the Universe" miniseries that came out&nbsp;
in 2018. It took place in the Injustice Universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:01.440
where Superman became the dictator of Earth. The&nbsp;
story saw several DC heroes journey to Eternia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:06.480
recruit He-Man to fight the Man of Steel.
While He-Man's mystical power allowed him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:11.120
to pummel Superman, some manipulation&nbsp;
by Skeletor caused He-Man to lose his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:15.920
connection to Grayskull's magic. This caused&nbsp;
him to revert back to Prince Adam, leaving him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.920
at the mercy of Superman, who considered&nbsp;
him less of a threat than Captain Carrot.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:25.840
He-Man is often compared to Conan the Barbarian,&nbsp;
but there's one other superhero he shares a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:32.720
in common with — Billy Batson, a.k.a the DC hero&nbsp;
known as Shazam or Captain Marvel. Like Billy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:38.160
Prince Adam is a young man imbued with mystical&nbsp;
powers that allow him to become a mighty hero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:42.240
Superman himself made this connection&nbsp;
in the "Injustice vs. Masters of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.640
Universe" miniseries, and fortunately&nbsp;
for Adam, so did a certain Wizard.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:51.520
During the course of the story, Skeletor managed&nbsp;
to steal the Power of Shazam and attempted to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:56.720
it to conquer Eternia. Instead, he got himself&nbsp;
killed and inadvertently passed the power of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:02.560
gods to Prince Adam, who was summoned before the&nbsp;
Wizard as his new champion. By simply shouting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:08.080
"Shazam!" Adam transformed into a white-garbed&nbsp;
being with similarities to both Shazam and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:13.920
He-Man. Newly empowered, the Shazam He-Man made&nbsp;
short work of the corrupt Injustice Superman.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.640
It was a scene bursting with fanboy energy,&nbsp;
although He-Man later went back to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:23.520
standard look and powers. While this might&nbsp;
disappoint some crossover fans, when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:28.080
already the most powerful man in the universe,&nbsp;
being a super-strong barbarian is enough.

